1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Tmux, an open-source terminal multiplexer that can juggle multiple terminal sessions from a single window.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
It was created by Nicolas Marriott in 2007 after becoming frustrated with the limitations of GNU Screen,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
and is offered under the more permissive BSD license.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
As you can see here, I'm building some crappy software, and I have a terminal window open for a web server,

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
one for Postgres, one for my Nginx logs, one for HTOP, one for Nano, and one for Repel to say hello world.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
It's complete chaos, but things get a lot better if I run this tmux command first,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
which will summon this magic little status bar.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Now I can take all these terminals and combine them into a single window,

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
where they're organized in the status bar like an array.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
I can easily navigate between them without ever touching the mouse,

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
split them into panes both horizontally and vertically, and can give each one of them a custom name.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
And if I accidentally closed it, all is not lost because the session is still running in the background.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Also, if you're constantly switching between multiple failed side projects,

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
it's time consuming to manually resurrect a bunch of terminal windows to set up your environment.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The tmux can be controlled with scripting to define session settings,

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
as well as programmatically.

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
With just a few lines of bash code, we can instantly fire up multiple terminals for our project,

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
with descriptive names and fancy colors.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
But you don't even have to do it all from scratch because tmux has a bunch of pre-built plugins,

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
like Resurrect will restore your environment after reboot,

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
or use Powerline to customize it in ways that would make even the most flamboyant peacock jealous.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
To get started, install it on any Unix-like machine, including Mac OS, Linux, and WSL,

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
then open up a terminal window and start a new session.

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Go ahead and run any random process,

25
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
then create a new terminal window by hitting Ctrl Bravo, let go, then hit Charlie.

26
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
A new terminal is pushed onto the stack, and we can navigate between them with Ctrl B followed by their index number.

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
We can also navigate with the arrow keys, or split these terminals into panes with quote for vertical or percent for horizontal.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Pretty cool, but oh no, I just closed it.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
But it didn't actually die, it only detached from reality.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
If we open up the terminal, we can run tmux ls to list out all the active sessions.

31
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
When we find the one we're looking for, we can reattach to it and get back to work.

32
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
What's really awesome, though, is that if we create a bash script, we can do all this programmatically.

33
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Let's define a variable for session name, and then first check to see if that session already exists with the tmux has session command.

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
If it exists, then we can simply reattach to it.

35
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Otherwise, we'll use the new session command to create a new one, with the initial window handling our text editor.

36
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
From there, we can tell tmux to cd into the project directory and run BIM.

37
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Just kidding, we'll run nano because it's far superior.

38
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
But now let's open up another window called server.

39
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
It'll cd into that same project and run some JavaScript slop.

40
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
We might also need a window to run a docker container, another one for the database, and another one for logging.

41
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
That takes care of our windows, but let's also customize the color and appearance.

42
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And then finally, we'll use select window to focus on the editor first.

43
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Now open the terminal and run the script to work smarter and not harder.

44
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
This has been tmux in 100 seconds.

